$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The conviction under appeal</h2>
<p>The appellant was convicted on 13 October 2023 by the Additional Sessions Judge / Fast Track Special Court (POCSO), Roorkee, District Haridwar, in Special Sessions Trial No. 144 of 2022, for offences under Sections 3(a) and 4 of the POCSO Act, 2012, with Sections 363 and 366 of the Indian Penal Code. His appeal — CRJA No. 157 of 2023 — has been pending since. He had been in custody since 22 August 2022, which by the date of this order meant more than four years and two months inside.</p>
<p>His first application for suspension of sentence was rejected by a coordinate bench on 21 April 2026. The present order, passed by Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Alok Mahra on 16 November 2026 on IA No. 4 of 2026, took a different view on a fuller consideration of the record.</p>

<h2>The three pillars of the order</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The age record.</strong> School records placed the victim at seventeen years, nine months and nine days at the time of the incident — months short of the POCSO threshold, with the mother (PW-1) unable to recall whether a birth certificate had ever been obtained.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The relationship evidence.</strong> The victim&rsquo;s own statement described a love relationship with the appellant — a fact the State did not dispute in its factual submissions.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The custody clock.</strong> Over four years in custody with the appeal nowhere near final hearing.</div>
</div>
<p>On this material the Court held that a case for suspension of sentence and bail pending appeal was made out, allowed the application, suspended execution of the sentence during the pendency of the appeal, and directed release on a personal bond with two sureties of the like amount. The appeal itself was directed to be listed in due course.</p>

<h2>Age disputes and adolescent relationships: a recurring fault line</h2>
<p>Two features of this case recur across POCSO appellate dockets. The first is the age dispute: because the Act&rsquo;s application turns entirely on the victim being a child, the reliability of school records, the availability of a birth certificate and the hierarchy of age-proof documents under Section 94 of the Juvenile Justice Act frequently become the decisive battleground of the appeal. Where the recorded age sits within touching distance of eighteen, the margin of documentary error is itself an arguable ground.</p>
<p>The second is the adolescent-relationship fact pattern — prosecutions arising from elopements or relationships where the alleged victim describes the association as consensual. Conviction may still follow at trial because consent is legally irrelevant below eighteen, but at the suspension stage appellate courts do weigh the realistic prospects of the appeal, and such records read very differently from predatory-assault cases.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should note</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>A rejected suspension application is not the end. Length of custody accumulates, and a second application on a sharpened record — here, the precise arithmetic of the school-recorded age — can succeed where a general plea failed.</p>
  <p>Build the age challenge at trial, not just on appeal: summon the admission register, confront the parents on the birth certificate, and preserve the contradictions.</p>
  <p>Suspension orders carry no forecast of outcome. Counsel should prepare the appeal on the assumption that every finding remains to be defended or dismantled on the full record.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information. Observations at the suspension stage are expressly not findings on the merits of the pending appeal.</div>
